/*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*\
  membrane27_swro_energy_recovery -- WHERE THE ENERGY OF SEAWATER RO GOES.

  THE PLANT.  membrane01's seawater (35 g/L NaCl, 25 C), 16 m3/h at 2 bar
  after pretreatment, split between a HIGH-PRESSURE PUMP (to 55 bar) and the
  low-pressure side of a ROTARY PRESSURE EXCHANGER (the ERI PX class: a
  ceramic rotor whose ducts are filled in turn by the high-pressure brine,
  which pushes the seawater they held out at nearly its own pressure, and by
  low-pressure seawater, which pushes the spent brine to drain).  The
  pressurised seawater the exchanger returns is lifted the last 1.6 bar by
  a BOOSTER pump, joins the high-pressure pump's discharge, and the two feed
  ONE SW30HR-380 stage (2 vessels x 7 elements, the record
  data/standards/assets/SW30HR-380.dat).  The stage's brine is the
  exchanger's high-pressure inlet -- a recycle, cut at `brine`.

  THE EXCHANGER'S THREE NUMBERS are a maker's and are declared, never
  defaulted (Stover, Desalination 203 (2007) 168-175, the ranges he states
  for isobaric rotary devices): pressure-transfer efficiency 0.96 (95-98 %),
  leakage 0.015 (1-2 %), both the middle of their ranges; mixing 0.03, the
  LOW end of his 3-6 %, because under this unit's definition (the brine
  share of the pressurised outlet's VOLUME) 3 % on this brine (6.3 % w/w
  against a 3.3 % feed) already raises the feed's salinity 2.8 %, the top
  of the 1-3 % he quotes for that penalty; the middle of his mixing range
  would give 4.3 %.  His two ranges are not consistent under this
  definition at 47 % recovery -- a finding about the definition, recorded
  and not tuned away.

  THE ANSWER (read off the run, 2026-11-01):
      plant recovery        47.2 % of the RO feed (16.2 m3/h), 48.3 % of
                            the seawater drawn (16.4 m3/h); permeate 7.94
                            m3/h, 99.23 % observed NaCl rejection
      brine                 8.31 m3/h at 54.12 bar, 6.3 % w/w
      exchanger             P_HPout 53.37 bar (solved from eta): 51.4 bar
                            recovered on 8.18 m3/h of seawater, 11.7 kW
                            hydraulic; HP-side loss 0.76 bar; feed
                            salinity 3.30 -> 3.39 % w/w (+2.83 %); flows
                            balanced to 1.0 % (LP in 8.39 vs HP in 8.31
                            m3/h); dissipated 0.52 kW
      high-pressure pump    14.47 kW shaft (8.06 m3/h, 2 -> 55 bar, eta 0.82)
      booster               0.46 kW shaft (8.18 m3/h, 53.37 -> 55 bar, eta 0.80)

  THE SPECIFIC ENERGY, DECOMPOSED (pump SHAFT powers over the permeate; no
  motor or drive efficiency is modelled, no pretreatment, no permeate
  delivery):
      with the exchanger      (14.47 + 0.46) kW / 7.94 m3/h  =  1.88 kWh/m3
      WITHOUT it, the same plant pumping its whole RO feed (16.24 m3/h,
      8.06 + 8.18) through the high-pressure pump and throttling the brine:
                              (16.24/3600) x 53e5 Pa / 0.82 = 29.2 kW
                                                     -> 3.67 kWh/m3
      so the device halves the plant's pumping energy, at the price of
      2.8 % more salinity on the membrane's feed.
      THE THERMODYNAMIC MINIMUM for this feed and this recovery, from the
      osmotic pressure the package gives (the module's own van't Hoff
      model, pi = 2 c R T; c = 9.568 kmol/h NaCl in 16.45 m3/h = 0.582
      kmol/m3, so pi_0 = 28.8 bar) at the plant recovery r = 0.483:
          w_min = pi_0 (-ln(1 - r)) / r = 28.8 x 1.366 = 39.3 bar
                = 3.93 MJ/m3 = 1.09 kWh/m3.
      The 0.79 kWh/m3 between the minimum and the plant is the membrane's
      overpressure (55 bar fed against 28.8-54 bar of osmotic pressure
      along the vessel), the pumps' efficiencies, the exchanger's 4 % and
      its booster.  A student should move the pressure, the recovery (the
      split fraction) and the three device numbers and watch the terms.

  THE KNIFE EDGE, which is real.  The exchanger returns 98.5 % of the
  brine's VOLUME to the membrane feed, so the loop's gain is 0.985 and the
  recovery is set by the 1.5 % that leaks and by how the permeate moves
  with the feed: the high-pressure pump's flow IS the permeate (plus the
  leak), and the split fraction here (0.49 to the pump) was tuned by hand
  until the device ran balanced -- what a real train's control does with
  that pump's drive.  Newton on the tear overshoots on such a loop (a trial
  brine with no water, a booster asked to pump down); system/solverDict
  selects Wegstein with its full secant step, which converges in five.

  WHAT THE RUN SAYS AND CANNOT SAY.  The mixer `Join` is DECLARED
  isothermal at the feed temperature: an adiabatic mix would need NaCl's
  liquid heat capacity, which its record does not carry (membrane01's
  component set), so the pumps' 0.28 K and 0.01 K dissipation rises are
  discarded there and no mixing duty is priced -- announced.  FOR THE SAME
  REASON THE PLANT'S FIRST LAW IS UNAVAILABLE on this component set
  (`energyBalance UNAVAILABLE`, as on membrane01): the exchanger's own row
  closes by construction (T unchanged, Q = 0), but nothing here can show
  it, and the pumps' useful work v dP is not carried by the liquid enthalpy
  surface in any case (Pump.cpp says why).  An ion-written twin (water, Na,
  Cl) would have a first law and would show the pumps' residual; it is not
  built here.  The exchanger's three balances ARE checked, independently
  of the engine, by bin/curate/check_pressure_exchanger.py on this case's
  result JSON.

  THE SHEET.  system/postDict sizes the exchanger (design/PX/
  pressureExchanger): one rotor at a DECLARED rated flow of 60 m3/h per
  unit (an author-set order of magnitude for the PX-Q300 class, to be
  replaced by a maker's sheet), the pressure drops and the hydraulic
  booster duty read from the unit, NOT COSTED (no Turton set).  The GUI
  draws it: the rotor nominal, the four ports with their pressures, the
  booster beside the high-pressure outlet.

  NOT a validation: no number here is measured.  NOT modelled: the rotor's
  hydraulics, the duct-by-duct unsteadiness, any dependence of the mixing on
  flow imbalance or salinity, the motor and drive, pretreatment.
\*---------------------------------------------------------------------------*/

tearStreams ( brine );

units
(
    {
        name        Split;
        type        splitter;
        in          feed;
        outputs     ( toHPpump  toPX );
        operation   { fractions ( 0.49  0.51 ); }     // tuned so the exchanger runs balanced (see header)
    }
    {
        name        HPpump;
        type        pump;
        in          toHPpump;
        outputs     ( hpFeed );
        operation
        {
            P_out     55 bar;                          // the membrane feed header
            eta       0.82;                            // ASSUMED: a large SWRO high-pressure pump
        }
    }
    {
        name        PX;
        type        pressureExchanger;
        inputs      ( brine  toPX );                   // highPressureIn = the brine, lowPressureIn = the seawater
        outputs     ( pxHPout  spentBrine );           // highPressureOut, lowPressureOut
        operation
        {
            efficiency      0.96;                      // Stover 2007: 95-98 % for the rotary ceramic class
            mixing          0.03;                      // volumetric, 3-6 %
            leakage         0.015;                     // lubrication flow, 1-2 %
            P_LPout         1.3 bar;                   // the drain line, absolute
            targetPressure  55 bar;                    // the HP pump's discharge: what the booster must reach
            source          "R. L. Stover, Desalination 203 (2007) 168-175, the ranges stated for isobaric rotary devices; efficiency and leakage the middle of their ranges, the mixing the low end (see the header); author-set";
        }
    }
    {
        name        Booster;
        type        pump;
        in          pxHPout;
        outputs     ( boosted );
        operation
        {
            P_out     55 bar;                          // rejoins the HP pump's discharge
            eta       0.80;                            // ASSUMED
        }
    }
    {
        name        Join;
        type        mixer;
        inputs      ( hpFeed  boosted );
        outputs     ( roFeed );
        operation   { T 298.15 K; }
    }
    {
        name        RO;
        type        spiralWoundModule;
        in          roFeed;
        outputs     ( brine  permeate );
        operation
        {
            module           SW30HR-380;               // data/standards/assets/, kind membraneModule
            vessels          2;                        // pressure vessels in parallel
            elements         7;                        // elements in series in each vessel
            P_permeate       1.0 bar;
            nNodes           20;                       // stations per element

            massTransfer
            {
                model          SchockMiquel;
                viscosity      0.95e-3;                // Pa.s, seawater near 25 C
            }
            pressureDrop
            {
                model          SchockMiquel;
            }
            polarisation
            {
                suctionCorrection  GeraldesAfonso2006;
                ionCoupling        none;               // one lumped salt
            }
        }
    }
);
